  <!-- WHAT IS DISTILLS -->
  <section id="what" class="bg-dark">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="shift-head on-dark">
        <span class="eyebrow" style="color:var(--orange-400);"><span class="dot"></span>THE SUBJECT</span>
        <h2>So what is Distills?</h2>
        <p>An AI-powered professional identity platform. You upload your career documents; it builds a hosted profile that visitors can have a conversation with. The profile answers in your voice, from your material, and admits when it doesn't know something.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="pipeline">
        <div class="pipe-step">
          <span class="ps-n">Step 01</span>
          <h4>Upload</h4>
          <p>Resume, CV, case studies, bio — PDF, DOCX, or pasted text.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pipe-step">
          <span class="ps-n">Step 02</span>
          <h4>Extract</h4>
          <p>Claude turns the documents into a structured knowledge base — not a resume, a set of typed, tagged facts and stories.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pipe-step">
          <span class="ps-n">Step 03</span>
          <h4>Interview</h4>
          <p>A conversational agent probes the gaps and thin spots, then folds the answers back into the knowledge base.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pipe-step">
          <span class="ps-n">Step 04</span>
          <h4>Publish</h4>
          <p>A live profile anyone can question — answering in first person, grounded only in what's in the knowledge base.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="stack-row">
        <span class="stack-pill">Cloudflare Workers</span>
        <span class="stack-pill">Cloudflare Pages</span>
        <span class="stack-pill">D1 · R2 · KV</span>
        <span class="stack-pill">Claude API</span>
        <span class="stack-pill">Stripe</span>
        <span class="stack-pill">vanilla HTML/CSS/JS</span>
      </div>

      <div class="remember on-dark-rm center" style="margin-top:44px;">
        <span class="rm-label">★ The one artifact to look at</span>
        <h3>The whole product is a JSONL file</h3>
        <p>Every surface downstream — the interview, the public chatbot, the job-description analysis — is retrieval and rendering over one line-delimited JSON file per person. If you understand the schema, you understand the product.</p>
        <p class="rm-sub">We'll put a real one on screen. It is the highest-value thing you'll look at today.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="codeblock" style="max-width:900px;margin:0 auto;">
        <span class="cb-label">knowledge.jsonl — one entry per line</span>
<pre>&#123;<span class="c-key">"id"</span>: <span class="c-str">"rag-2026-08-26-004"</span>,
 <span class="c-key">"type"</span>: <span class="c-str">"narrative"</span>,
 <span class="c-key">"topic"</span>: <span class="c-str">"career_history"</span>,
 <span class="c-key">"confidence"</span>: <span class="c-str">"verified"</span>,        <span class="c-com">// or "inferred" — the model flags its own guesses</span>
 <span class="c-key">"source"</span>: <span class="c-str">"document_upload"</span>,
 <span class="c-key">"tags"</span>: [<span class="c-str">"platform_migration"</span>, <span class="c-str">"team_leadership"</span>],
 <span class="c-key">"title"</span>: <span class="c-str">"Migrating the billing platform"</span>,
 <span class="c-key">"content"</span>: <span class="c-str">"Led a team of six through a nine-month..."</span>&#125;</pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- THE SIX SURFACES -->
  <section id="surfaces">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="shift-head">
        <span class="eyebrow on-light"><span class="dot"></span>THE WALKTHROUGH · 22 MIN</span>
        <h2>Six surfaces, and what's really behind each one</h2>
        <p>At each stop the question is the same: <em>what's the AI doing here?</em> Guess first. Then open the panel. Each one is tagged with the exam domains it exercises.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="acc-toolbar">
        <button class="expand-all" id="expandAll" type="button">Expand all</button>
      </div>

      <div class="acc-list">

        <!-- SURFACE 1 -->
        <details class="acc">
          <summary>
            <span class="acc-n">01</span>
            <span>
              <span class="acc-title">Document → Knowledge Base</span>
              <span class="acc-kicker">Everyone assumes this is "parsing a resume." It isn't.</span>
            </span>
            <span class="acc-chev">⌄</span>
          </summary>
          <div class="acc-body">
            <div class="dom-row">
              <span class="dom-badge is-primary"><span class="dn">D4</span> Prompt Engineering &amp; Structured Output</span>
              <span class="dom-badge"><span class="dn">D5</span> Context Management</span>
            </div>

            <h5>What you see</h5>
            <p>You drop in a PDF. A few seconds later the product knows things about you.</p>

            <h5>What it actually does</h5>
            <p>It does not extract fields. It asks Claude to <strong>classify and decompose</strong> the document into typed knowledge entries — 8 to 12 per document — each of which must be <strong>self-contained</strong>, readable without any other entry.</p>
            <ul>
              <li><code>type</code> — <code>fact</code>, <code>narrative</code>, <code>qa_pair</code>, <code>technical</code>, or <code>fit_assessment</code></li>
              <li><code>topic</code> — snake_case category (<code>career_history</code>, <code>skills</code>, <code>values</code>…)</li>
              <li><code>confidence</code> — <code>verified</code> if directly stated, <code>inferred</code> if the model concluded it</li>
              <li><code>tags</code> — 2–5 keywords, required, never empty</li>
            </ul>
            <p>The instructions are opinionated: <em>split compound claims into separate entries; write the Q&amp;A questions the way a recruiter would actually ask them; be specific — include numbers, technologies, timeframes.</em></p>

            <div class="ask-room">
              <span class="ar-label">Ask the room</span>
              <p>Why would you make the model label its own confidence?</p>
              <p class="answer">Because a downstream consumer needs to know whether to trust it — and because it gives the human something concrete to correct. An inferred claim presented as fact is how a profile quietly starts lying about someone.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="ask-room" style="border-style:solid;border-color:var(--line-100);">
              <span class="ar-label">The honest critique</span>
              <p>The schema is requested <strong>in the prompt</strong> — "output only valid JSONL, one object per line." If one line comes back malformed, that entry is silently lost. The exam's answer is <code>tool_use</code> with a JSON schema, which guarantees the shape. That's a real gap, and it's Domain 4 sitting in production.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </details>

        <!-- SURFACE 2 -->
        <details class="acc">
          <summary>
            <span class="acc-n">02</span>
            <span>
              <span class="acc-title">The Interview — a stateful agent loop</span>
              <span class="acc-kicker">Three phases, enforced entirely by prose.</span>
            </span>
            <span class="acc-chev">⌄</span>
          </summary>
          <div class="acc-body">
            <div class="dom-row">
              <span class="dom-badge is-primary"><span class="dn">D1</span> Agentic Architecture &amp; Orchestration</span>
              <span class="dom-badge"><span class="dn">D5</span> Context Management</span>
            </div>

            <h5>What you see</h5>
            <p>A conversation that seems to know what it already asked, and doesn't repeat itself.</p>

            <h5>What it actually is</h5>
            <p>One system prompt running a <strong>three-phase script the user is never told about</strong>:</p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Phase 1 — Clarification</strong> (3–5 exchanges): probe anything ambiguous or thin in the uploaded documents.</li>
              <li><strong>Phase 2 — Structured discovery</strong> (10–20 exchanges): STAR-format behavioural questions across experience, leadership, technical depth, collaboration style, differentiators, values.</li>
              <li><strong>Phase 3 — Wrap-up</strong>: transition warmly, ask what was missed, mine the answer, close and point at the generate button.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>The knowledge base is injected into the system prompt, and the intake-form answers are injected with an explicit instruction: <em>already captured — DO NOT ask about these again.</em> There is also a rule that it must never say the word "Phase" out loud.</p>

            <h5>The part that matters</h5>
            <p><strong>The phases are enforced by instruction, not by code.</strong> There is no state machine. The model is trusted to count its own exchanges and transition on its own.</p>

            <div class="ask-room">
              <span class="ar-label">Ask the room</span>
              <p>What happens if it doesn't transition?</p>
              <p class="answer">Nothing catches it. This is the cleanest "prompt-based guidance vs. programmatic enforcement" example in the whole app — and Domain 1 is 27% of the exam.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </details>

        <!-- SURFACE 3 -->
        <details class="acc">
          <summary>
            <span class="acc-n">03</span>
            <span>
              <span class="acc-title">The Public Profile Chatbot — RAG, and simpler than you'd guess</span>
              <span class="acc-kicker">No embeddings. No vector database. Keyword scoring.</span>
            </span>
            <span class="acc-chev">⌄</span>
          </summary>
          <div class="acc-body">
            <div class="dom-row">
              <span class="dom-badge is-primary"><span class="dn">D5</span> Context Management &amp; Reliability</span>
              <span class="dom-badge"><span class="dn">D4</span> Prompt Engineering</span>
              <span class="dom-badge"><span class="dn">D1</span> Orchestration</span>
            </div>

            <h5>What you see</h5>
            <p>A live profile. Anyone with the link asks a question and gets an answer in first person, referencing real projects.</p>

            <h5>What it actually is</h5>
            <p>Retrieval, then generation. Strip the stopwords out of the question, score every knowledge-base entry, take the <strong>top 8</strong>, put them in the system prompt.</p>
            <div class="codeblock">
              <span class="cb-label">the entire retrieval algorithm</span>
<pre><span class="c-com">// match in the entry body</span>       +3
<span class="c-com">// match in topic or title</span>      +2
<span class="c-com">// match in a tag</span>              +1
<span class="c-com">// match in a Q&amp;A question</span>     +5   <span class="c-com">← questions are the best signal</span>
<span class="c-com">// entry marked "verified"</span>      +1</pre>
            </div>
            <p>That's it. No embeddings, no similarity search, no vector store. It's about forty lines of JavaScript.</p>

            <h5>The AI moment isn't retrieval — it's the guardrail</h5>
            <p>The system prompt mandates an <strong>exact refusal phrase</strong>: <em>"If the knowledge base doesn't have enough to answer, you MUST use this exact phrase: 'I don't have details on that in my profile yet.' Do not paraphrase it."</em></p>

            <h5>Then look at why</h5>
            <p>A function watches the outgoing response for that phrase. When it matches, the question gets logged as a <strong>knowledge gap</strong> and surfaces on the owner's dashboard: <em>"here's what visitors asked that your profile couldn't answer."</em> The failure mode is instrumented and sold back to the user as a feature.</p>

            <div class="ask-room">
              <span class="ar-label">We'll do this live</span>
              <p>We ask the demo profile something deliberately out of scope, watch it decline, then open the gap dashboard. Ninety seconds, and it reframes the whole class.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="ask-room" style="border-style:solid;border-color:var(--line-100);">
              <span class="ar-label">The honest critique</span>
              <p>Gap detection is a list of <strong>sixteen substring matches</strong> hoping to catch every way a model might phrase "I don't know." That's analytics data resting on string matching. The architect's answer: have the model emit a structured signal instead of parsing its own prose.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </details>

        <!-- SURFACE 4 -->
        <details class="acc">
          <summary>
            <span class="acc-n">04</span>
            <span>
              <span class="acc-title">Job Description Mode — enum design you can see</span>
              <span class="acc-kicker">The product's tone is enforced by an enum and two caps.</span>
            </span>
            <span class="acc-chev">⌄</span>
          </summary>
          <div class="acc-body">
            <div class="dom-row">
              <span class="dom-badge is-primary"><span class="dn">D4</span> Structured Output</span>
              <span class="dom-badge"><span class="dn">D5</span> Context Management</span>
            </div>

            <h5>What you see</h5>
            <p>Paste in a job description. Get back an honest read on where you fit and where you don't.</p>

            <h5>What it actually produces</h5>
            <p>More JSONL — 3 to 5 entries of type <code>fit_assessment</code>, appended to the same knowledge base, with <code>fit_type</code> constrained to exactly three values:</p>
            <ul>
              <li><code>good_fit</code> — specific experience that directly matches a requirement</li>
              <li><code>not_ideal</code> — a real gap. <strong>Capped at 2.</strong> Instruction: <em>be honest, constructive, not discouraging.</em></li>
              <li><code>red_flag</code> — a serious mismatch. <strong>Capped at 1</strong>, "use sparingly and only if clearly warranted."</li>
            </ul>

            <h5>The part that matters</h5>
            <p><strong>The product's bedside manner is implemented as an enum plus two numbers.</strong> Not a style guide, not a tone document — a constraint in a schema. That's a design decision you can point at.</p>
            <p>Note what it does <em>not</em> produce: there is no match score, no percentage, no grade. Nobody has to defend a number.</p>

            <div class="ask-room">
              <span class="ar-label">Ask the room</span>
              <p>The job description is truncated at 3,000 characters and the knowledge base at 35 entries. What breaks?</p>
              <p class="answer">A long job posting silently loses its tail — which is frequently where the actual requirements live, after the culture boilerplate. Silent truncation is a genuine production failure mode and it's on the exam.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="ask-room" style="border-style:solid;border-color:var(--line-100);">
              <span class="ar-label">The exam angle</span>
              <p>The blueprint says: give enums an <code>"other"</code> escape hatch, and an <code>"unclear"</code> value for when the model genuinely can't classify — an honest escape beats a confident wrong label. <code>fit_type</code> has neither. So what does it do when a requirement maps to none of the three? <strong>That's an exam question sitting in production.</strong></p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </details>

        <!-- SURFACE 5 -->
        <details class="acc">
          <summary>
            <span class="acc-n">05</span>
            <span>
              <span class="acc-title">Model Routing &amp; Rate Limits</span>
              <span class="acc-kicker">Two different Claude models, on purpose. This is the business.</span>
            </span>
            <span class="acc-chev">⌄</span>
          </summary>
          <div class="acc-body">
            <div class="dom-row">
              <span class="dom-badge is-primary"><span class="dn">D5</span> Reliability &amp; Cost</span>
              <span class="dom-badge"><span class="dn">D1</span> Orchestration</span>
            </div>

            <h5>What you see</h5>
            <p>Three tiers. Different features unlock at different prices.</p>

            <h5>What's actually being gated</h5>
            <p>Not features — <strong>inference</strong>. Two models are in play, deliberately:</p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Sonnet</strong> for the heavy pipeline — document extraction, the interview, job-description analysis. Runs rarely, matters enormously, worth paying for.</li>
              <li><strong>Haiku</strong> for the public chatbot. Runs constantly, needs to be fast, cheap enough to give away.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>And the daily query caps are per tier — free profiles get 20 queries a day and 5 per visitor; paid tiers get 100/25 and 200/50.</p>

            <h5>The line to read out loud</h5>
            <p>There is a comment in the source next to those limits: <em>"At $0.006/query: Pro ($24/mo) can sustain ~3,000 queries/day before cost exceeds revenue."</em></p>
            <p><strong>Cost per inference determined the model choice, which determined the rate limit, which determined the price of the tier.</strong> The whole business, in one line of arithmetic.</p>

            <div class="ask-room" style="border-style:solid;border-color:var(--orange-500);">
              <span class="ar-label">Now check that comment — it's wrong</span>
              <p>Do the arithmetic. It takes twenty seconds and it does not come out.</p>
              <div class="codeblock" style="margin:12px 0 14px;">
<pre><span class="c-com">// the claim</span>
3,000 queries/<span class="c-key">day</span> × 30 days × $0.006  =  <span class="c-str">$540/month</span>
                             revenue  =  <span class="c-str">$24/month</span>
                                          <span class="c-com">→ a 22× loss, not break-even</span>

<span class="c-com">// what makes it work</span>
3,000 queries/<span class="c-key">month</span>       × $0.006  =  <span class="c-str">$18/month</span>   <span class="c-com">✓  ≈ 100/day</span></pre>
              </div>
              <p>Somebody wrote <strong>"day"</strong> where they meant <strong>"month."</strong> The number has sat in the codebase ever since, quietly telling every future reader that the tier has thirty times more headroom than it actually has.</p>
              <p>The real figures, on Haiku at roughly 3,300 tokens in and 300 out — about <strong>$0.0048 per query</strong>:</p>
              <ul>
                <li>At 40 queries/day: <strong>$5.78/month</strong> against $24 revenue — a healthy <strong>$18.22</strong> margin</li>
                <li>True break-even: <strong>166 queries/day</strong>, not 3,000</li>
                <li>Same profile on Sonnet: <strong>$17.35/month</strong> — margin drops to $6.65</li>
                <li>Same profile on Opus: <strong>$28.92/month</strong> — <strong>underwater at −$4.92</strong></li>
              </ul>
              <p class="answer">Nobody was careless here. The model was right, the price was right, the units were wrong. This is what an unverified number looks like in production — Week 3's benchmark lesson, pointed at our own code. Go find the break-even yourself in the calculator below.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </details>

        <!-- SURFACE 6 -->
        <details class="acc is-twist">
          <summary>
            <span class="acc-n">06</span>
            <span>
              <span class="acc-title">The Profile Score — the one that isn't AI at all</span>
              <span class="acc-kicker">Zero model calls. It's regular expressions.</span>
            </span>
            <span class="acc-chev">⌄</span>
          </summary>
          <div class="acc-body">
            <div class="dom-row">
              <span class="dom-badge is-primary"><span class="dn">D1</span> Architecture &amp; Tradeoffs</span>
              <span class="dom-badge"><span class="dn">D5</span> Cost &amp; Latency</span>
            </div>

            <h5>What you see</h5>
            <p>Paste a resume, get a score out of 100 across four dimensions — completeness, STAR-story coverage, recruiter red flags, ATS compatibility. It feels like the AI just read your resume.</p>

            <h5>What it actually is</h5>
            <p><strong>Pure regular expressions. Not one API call.</strong></p>
            <ul>
              <li>Counts buzzwords with a regex — <code>hard-working</code>, <code>team player</code>, <code>self-starter</code>, <code>detail-oriented</code> — and deducts points past a threshold</li>
              <li>Tests whether a <code>%</code> or <code>$</code> appears near an action verb, and calls that "quantifiable impact"</li>
              <li>Checks for a standard email pattern and the absence of table markup, and calls that "ATS compatible"</li>
            </ul>

            <div class="ask-room">
              <span class="ar-label">Ask the room — and let it sit</span>
              <p>Is that dishonest? Or is it good engineering?</p>
              <p class="answer">The defensible answer: it's instant, free, deterministic, and it lives at the top of the funnel where volume is highest and stakes are lowest. Spending inference there would be slower, cost more, and be no more accurate — with the added downside that the same resume could score differently twice.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="remember" style="margin-top:22px;">
              <span class="rm-label">★ Remember this</span>
              <h3>Knowing when <em>not</em> to call the model is an architecture skill</h3>
              <p>The exam frames this as a decision with four tests — complexity, value, viability, and cost of error. If the answer to any of them is no, stay at the simpler tier. Most of the AI slideware you'll be pitched this year fails at least two.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </details>

      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

