      <div class="acc-list" style="margin:44px auto 0;">
        <details class="acc">
          <summary>
            <span class="acc-n">D2</span>
            <span>
              <span class="acc-title">The missing domain — what one tool would you add?</span>
              <span class="acc-kicker">18% of the exam, and 0% of this application.</span>
            </span>
            <span class="acc-chev">⌄</span>
          </summary>
          <div class="acc-body">
            <p>Everything in Distills is a single-shot prompt or a fixed pipeline. Nothing decides to go and get something. Meanwhile the blueprint spends nearly a fifth of the exam on tool interfaces — because tool descriptions are how the model decides what to reach for, and vague descriptions cause misrouting.</p>

            <h5>Strong answers</h5>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Fetch the job description from a URL</strong> — instead of asking the user to paste and silently truncating at 3,000 characters</li>
              <li><strong>Verify a claim against its source document</strong> — the difference between "the profile says this" and "the resume supports this"</li>
              <li><strong>Read a portfolio or repository</strong> — pull evidence the resume only alludes to</li>
            </ul>

            <h5>A weak answer, and why it's worth discussing</h5>
            <p>"Search the internet." The description is too vague for the model to know <em>when</em> to reach for it, so it either never fires or fires constantly. That's the exact failure the exam tests — and the fix is renaming and rescoping the tool, not adding a rule to the system prompt.</p>

            <div class="ask-room">
              <span class="ar-label">Bring this to the discussion</span>
              <p>Don't just name a tool. Say what its <strong>description</strong> would be — because that's the part the model actually reads.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- BONUS: PROFILE AS AN ENDPOINT -->
  <section id="endpoint" class="bg-dark">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="shift-head on-dark">
        <span class="eyebrow" style="color:var(--orange-400);"><span class="dot"></span>BONUS CONVERSATION · 5 MIN</span>
        <h2>What if the thing asking the questions isn't a person?</h2>
        <p>None of this is built. It's a thought experiment — and it's the most useful forward-looking argument this teardown makes.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="remember on-dark-rm" style="max-width:none;margin-top:0;">
        <span class="rm-label">The thesis</span>
        <p>Today a human clicks a link and asks a profile questions. The obvious next step is that the caller is <strong>an agent</strong> — a recruiter's agent screening for a role, querying a hundred candidate profiles directly. Each one answers in that candidate's voice, from that candidate's material, refusing where the material runs out.</p>
        <p class="rm-sub">Every professional becomes a queryable endpoint that answers as themselves — and gets told what was asked.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="acc-list" style="max-width:none;">

        <details class="acc" style="background:rgba(255,255,255,.05);border-color:var(--line-dark);">
          <summary>
            <span class="acc-n" style="color:var(--orange-400);border-color:var(--orange-500);">01</span>
            <span>
              <span class="acc-title" style="color:#fff;">Get the terminology right — the room will conflate these</span>
              <span class="acc-kicker" style="color:#9FB4B8;">"Agent-to-agent" is the wrong phrase, and the right one is stronger.</span>
            </span>
            <span class="acc-chev">⌄</span>
          </summary>
          <div class="acc-body" style="border-top-color:var(--line-dark);">
            <ul style="color:#C4D2D4;">
              <li style="color:#C4D2D4;"><strong style="color:#fff;">MCP is client/server.</strong> The recruiter's agent is the <em>client</em>. The profile is the <em>server</em>. There is no peer agent sitting on the candidate's side.</li>
              <li style="color:#C4D2D4;"><strong style="color:#fff;">Domain 1</strong> covers multi-agent orchestration in the coordinator-and-subagent sense — one system, one context, the <code style="background:rgba(255,255,255,.08);border:none;color:var(--orange-400);">Task</code> tool. That is <em>not</em> this.</li>
              <li style="color:#C4D2D4;"><strong style="color:#fff;">Cross-organisation interop</strong> over MCP is Domain 2 territory, and it's more speculative than anything in the exam's scenario bank.</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="color:#C4D2D4;">Use the precise version — it's also the more defensible claim. Not "two agents talking," but "a professional identity that any agent can query, that still speaks for itself."</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="acc is-twist" style="background:rgba(255,255,255,.05);border-color:var(--orange-500);">
          <summary>
            <span class="acc-n">02</span>
            <span>
              <span class="acc-title" style="color:#fff;">The design decision worth arguing about</span>
              <span class="acc-kicker" style="color:#9FB4B8;">Two tools. One of them quietly destroys the product.</span>
            </span>
            <span class="acc-chev">⌄</span>
          </summary>
          <div class="acc-body" style="border-top-color:var(--line-dark);">
            <p style="color:#C4D2D4;">Say you're exposing one tool to the outside world. Which?</p>
            <div class="codeblock">
<pre><span class="c-key">search_knowledge_base(query)</span>
  <span class="c-com">→ returns raw KB entries</span>
  <span class="c-com">→ the CALLING agent does the synthesis —</span>
  <span class="c-com">  its voice, its framing, its inferences</span>

<span class="c-key">ask_profile(slug, question)</span>
  <span class="c-com">→ returns the generated answer</span>
  <span class="c-com">→ voice, refusal phrase and gap logging all preserved</span></pre>
            </div>
            <p style="color:#C4D2D4;"><strong style="color:#fff;">Here's the trap.</strong> Voice, the mandated refusal, and the gap analytics all live in the chat route — <em>not</em> in retrieval. Expose retrieval and Distills becomes a <strong style="color:#fff;">data source</strong>. Expose the conversation and it stays a <strong style="color:#fff;">representative</strong>.</p>
            <p style="color:#C4D2D4;">For a product whose entire promise is "it speaks as you," that's the whole ballgame. It's the Surface 03 lesson — the guardrail <em>is</em> the product — arriving from the opposite direction.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="acc" style="background:rgba(255,255,255,.05);border-color:var(--line-dark);">
          <summary>
            <span class="acc-n" style="color:var(--orange-400);border-color:var(--orange-500);">03</span>
            <span>
              <span class="acc-title" style="color:#fff;">The tool set you'd actually design</span>
              <span class="acc-kicker" style="color:#9FB4B8;">Three of these are thin wrappers. One of them is the real work.</span>
            </span>
            <span class="acc-chev">⌄</span>
          </summary>
          <div class="acc-body" style="border-top-color:var(--line-dark);">
            <div class="codeblock">
<pre><span class="c-key">ask_profile</span>(slug, question)      <span class="c-com">// wraps the existing chat route</span>
<span class="c-key">get_profile_summary</span>(slug)        <span class="c-com">// cheap orienting call — don't burn context</span>
<span class="c-key">assess_fit</span>(slug, jd_text)        <span class="c-com">// the JD-fit prompt already exists</span>
<span class="c-key">find_candidates</span>(criteria)        <span class="c-com">// ...this one doesn't exist at all</span></pre>
            </div>
            <p style="color:#C4D2D4;"><strong style="color:#fff;">Be honest about the last one.</strong> Cross-profile search is the piece the whole "agents finding ideal fits" story depends on, and it's by far the biggest build. Naming that gap out loud is the difference between a roadmap and a pitch deck.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="acc" style="background:rgba(255,255,255,.05);border-color:var(--line-dark);">
          <summary>
            <span class="acc-n" style="color:var(--orange-400);border-color:var(--orange-500);">04</span>
            <span>
              <span class="acc-title" style="color:#fff;">Three questions nobody has answered</span>
              <span class="acc-kicker" style="color:#9FB4B8;">All unsolved in the app today. All Domain 2.</span>
            </span>
            <span class="acc-chev">⌄</span>
          </summary>
          <div class="acc-body" style="border-top-color:var(--line-dark);">
            <ul style="color:#C4D2D4;">
              <li style="color:#C4D2D4;"><strong style="color:#fff;">Consent.</strong> Public chat is open to anyone with the link. Agent traffic at scale turns that into a scraping surface. Should a profile opt into <em>agent</em> access separately from <em>human</em> access?</li>
              <li style="color:#C4D2D4;"><strong style="color:#fff;">Rate limits break.</strong> The quotas are per-profile-per-day and per-IP-per-day — a design that assumes a human in a browser. Agent traffic arrives either from one address (legitimate agents blocked instantly) or from many (quota trivially bypassed).</li>
              <li style="color:#C4D2D4;"><strong style="color:#fff;">Who's asking?</strong> A recruiter agent, a competitor, and a scraper are indistinguishable today. That's identity, vaults, and OAuth.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>

      <div class="remember on-dark-rm" style="max-width:none;margin-top:26px;">
        <span class="rm-label">★ The closer</span>
        <h3>"Three recruiter agents asked about Kubernetes this week — and your profile couldn't answer."</h3>
        <p>That feature only exists <strong>because</strong> you exposed the conversation instead of the data. The architecture decision and the product feature turn out to be the same decision.</p>
        <p class="rm-sub">If you take one thing out of today, take that.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- DISCUSSION -->
  <section id="discussion">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="shift-head">
        <span class="eyebrow on-light"><span class="dot"></span>DISCUSSION · 11 MIN</span>
        <h2>Now tear it apart</h2>
        <p>No defending the product. Bring what your training told you, and say what this app doesn't do.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="acc-list">
        <details class="acc">
          <summary>
            <span class="acc-n">Q</span>
            <span>
              <span class="acc-title">The prompts we'll actually use</span>
              <span class="acc-kicker">Come with an answer to at least one of these.</span>
            </span>
            <span class="acc-chev">⌄</span>
          </summary>
          <div class="acc-body">
            <ul>
              <li>Where would you <strong>add</strong> a model call that isn't there today?</li>
              <li>Where would you <strong>remove</strong> one? (Surface 06 gives you permission to think this way.)</li>
              <li>What felt templated versus intelligent — and what tipped you off? You now know one answer for certain. Find another.</li>
              <li>If you could add one tool, what would it be, and what would its description say?</li>
              <li>The job description gets truncated at 3,000 characters. <strong>How would you find out that's hurting you?</strong></li>
              <li>What's the weakest moment in the user flow? Most products have exactly one.</li>
              <li>If you've done the Skilljar courses: what did the training tell you to do that this app doesn't?</li>
            </ul>
