  <!-- HOMEWORK -->
  <section id="homework" class="bg-dark">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="shift-head on-dark">
        <span class="eyebrow" style="color:var(--orange-400);"><span class="dot"></span>DUE WEEK 5 · ONE HOUR</span>
        <h2>Build one core loop</h2>
        <p>Not an app. One loop. Pick which of the three you want, then pick your tier honestly — all three tiers are complete submissions, and the schema matters more than the interface.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="remember on-dark-rm" style="max-width:none;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:38px;">
        <span class="rm-label">Pick one loop</span>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Resume → three interview questions.</strong> Paste a resume, get three questions generated from what's actually in it.</li>
          <li><strong>Text → a reply in your voice.</strong> Type anything, get a response that sounds like you — your vocabulary, your domain, your way of hedging.</li>
          <li><strong>Job description → alignment analysis.</strong> Compare a posting against a resume and return the gaps as <code style="background:rgba(255,255,255,.08);color:var(--orange-400);border:none;">good_fit</code> / <code style="background:rgba(255,255,255,.08);color:var(--orange-400);border:none;">not_ideal</code> / <code style="background:rgba(255,255,255,.08);color:var(--orange-400);border:none;">red_flag</code>.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>

      <div class="tier-grid">
        <div class="tier">
          <span class="tr-n">TIER 1</span>
          <h4>The Prompt</h4>
          <div class="tr-time">~20 min · everyone does at least this</div>
          <p>Don't build an app. Build the thing an app would be built out of.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Write the <strong>system prompt</strong> and the <strong>JSON schema</strong></li>
            <li>Run it in Claude against your own resume</li>
            <li>Iterate three times</li>
            <li>Bring the schema, the first output, and the third</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="tier is-default">
          <span class="tr-n">TIER 2 · DEFAULT</span>
          <h4>The Artifact</h4>
          <div class="tr-time">~45 min · if you want something clickable</div>
          <p>Have Claude build your loop as a self-contained artifact that calls Claude from inside itself.</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>No hosting. No API key. No serverless function.</strong></li>
            <li>The share link is your live URL</li>
            <li>Give it your Tier 1 schema as the spec</li>
            <li>Fallback: a local HTML file, key pasted at runtime, screen-recorded — that counts too</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="tier">
          <span class="tr-n">TIER 3</span>
          <h4>The Real Thing</h4>
          <div class="tr-time">~60 min · only if you already have the accounts</div>
          <p>Claude Code, a static page, and one serverless function holding the key.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>This is a genuine full hour</li>
            <li>It's a <strong>Domain 3</strong> exercise as much as a build</li>
            <li>Write the CLAUDE.md first</li>
            <li>Use plan mode before you let it execute — then read the diff</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3 style="color:#fff;font-size:24px;margin-bottom:8px;">Hints — open the one you need</h3>
      <p style="color:#9FB4B8;font-size:15.5px;margin:0 0 22px;max-width:720px;">You do not have to read these. If you already know how you'd approach it, go. These exist so nobody stalls at a blank page for their whole hour.</p>

      <details class="hint">
        <summary><span class="h-ico">01</span>How to start if you have no idea how to start<span class="h-chev">⌄</span></summary>
        <div class="hint-body">
          <h6>Do this in order</h6>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Write down what "done" looks like as one sentence.</strong> Not "a resume tool." Something like: <em>"Paste resume text, get exactly three interview questions that each reference a specific thing in that resume."</em> If you can't write that sentence, nothing downstream will work.</li>
            <li><strong>Write the output shape before the prompt.</strong> What fields come back? What types? What's required? This is the schema, and it's the actual deliverable.</li>
            <li><strong>Write the system prompt as a job description</strong> for someone you just hired: what they do, what good looks like, what they must never do.</li>
            <li><strong>Run it once by hand</strong> in a Claude conversation against your own resume. Read the output like a critic.</li>
            <li><strong>Change exactly one thing</strong> and run it again. Not five things. One. Then you know what caused the difference.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>If you stop after step 5, you've completed Tier 1 and you've done the part the exam actually tests.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="hint">
        <summary><span class="h-ico">02</span>A starter schema you can steal (Loop 1 — interview questions)<span class="h-chev">⌄</span></summary>
        <div class="hint-body">
          <h6>Start here and change it</h6>
          <div class="codeblock" style="background:var(--teal-900);border:1px solid var(--line-dark);">
<pre>&#123;
  <span class="c-key">"questions"</span>: [
    &#123;
      <span class="c-key">"question"</span>:  <span class="c-str">"string"</span>,
      <span class="c-key">"targets"</span>:   <span class="c-str">"string"</span>,   <span class="c-com">// the specific résumé line it probes</span>
      <span class="c-key">"why"</span>:       <span class="c-str">"string"</span>,   <span class="c-com">// what a good answer would reveal</span>
      <span class="c-key">"category"</span>:  <span class="c-str">"leadership" | "technical" | "judgment" | "unclear"</span>
    &#125;
  ]
&#125;</pre>
          </div>
          <h6>Things worth noticing about it</h6>
          <ul>
            <li><code>targets</code> is the anti-hallucination field. If the model has to name the résumé line it's probing, it can't invent a question about a job you never had.</li>
            <li><code>category</code> includes <code>"unclear"</code> — the escape hatch the exam keeps asking about. Without it, an ambiguous question gets a confident wrong label.</li>
            <li>Nothing here is a number. Resist adding a score; you'd only have to defend it.</li>
          </ul>
          <h6>The stretch goal</h6>
          <p>Find an input that makes it return <code>"unclear"</code>. If you can't, your enum is doing less work than you think it is.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="hint">
        <summary><span class="h-ico">03</span>A system prompt skeleton (adapt to any of the three loops)<span class="h-chev">⌄</span></summary>
        <div class="hint-body">
          <div class="codeblock" style="background:var(--teal-900);border:1px solid var(--line-dark);">
<pre><span class="c-com">ROLE</span>
You are a [technical recruiter / interview coach / hiring manager]
reading a candidate's résumé.

<span class="c-com">TASK</span>
Produce exactly three interview questions grounded in this résumé.

<span class="c-com">RULES</span>
- Every question must reference something specifically stated.
- Do not ask anything answerable from the résumé itself.
- If the résumé is too thin to support three questions, say so
  rather than inventing material.
- Use only what is provided. Never infer employers, dates, or titles.

<span class="c-com">OUTPUT</span>
Return only JSON matching the provided schema. No preamble.</pre>
          </div>
          <h6>Why it's shaped like that</h6>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Role first</strong> — it sets vocabulary and standards more efficiently than any amount of adjectives.</li>
            <li><strong>"Do not ask anything answerable from the résumé"</strong> is the whole quality bar in one line. Without it you get "So you worked at Xbox?"</li>
            <li><strong>The thin-résumé rule is the important one.</strong> It's the same move as the refusal phrase in Surface 03 — you're pre-deciding what happens when there isn't enough material, instead of letting the model improvise.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="hint">
        <summary><span class="h-ico">04</span>Tier 2 — what to actually say to Claude<span class="h-chev">⌄</span></summary>
        <div class="hint-body">
          <p>Paste your Tier 1 work in and ask for the wrapper. Something close to this:</p>
          <div class="codeblock" style="background:var(--teal-900);border:1px solid var(--line-dark);">
<pre>Build me a single-page tool as an artifact.

It should:
- take pasted résumé text in a textarea
- send it to Claude using the system prompt below
- enforce the JSON schema below
- render the three questions as cards, each showing
  the question and which résumé line it targets
- show a clear message if the résumé is too thin

Keep it to one file. No build step. Make it look like
something I'd put on a portfolio, not a homework assignment.

SYSTEM PROMPT:
[paste yours]

SCHEMA:
[paste yours]</pre>
          </div>
          <h6>Then iterate like you did in Week 2</h6>
          <ul>
            <li>Critique it out loud before asking for a change — name the single worst thing about it.</li>
            <li>Ask for that one fix. Not a list of five.</li>
            <li>Try it with a deliberately terrible résumé. That's where you find out whether your thin-material rule works.</li>
          </ul>
          <p><strong>Verify the artifact can call Claude in your account before you build the whole thing.</strong> If it can't, switch to the local-file fallback immediately — don't spend forty minutes discovering it at the end.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="hint">
        <summary><span class="h-ico">05</span>"Should I use the Claude Agent SDK for this?" — no, and here's why<span class="h-chev">⌄</span></summary>
        <div class="hint-body">
          <p>Somebody always asks, and the reasoning is genuinely exam-relevant.</p>
          <p>The <strong>Claude Agent SDK</strong> is Claude Code packaged as a library. It ships built-in file, bash, and search tools, subagents, hooks, permissions, and the full agent loop — and <strong>you still host and deploy it yourself</strong>. It's built for agents that work across a filesystem over many turns.</p>
          <p><strong>Your homework is one prompt in, one structured object out.</strong> That's a single API call. Reaching for an agent harness to do a single-shot extraction is exactly the over-architecture the exam's "should I build an agent?" test exists to catch: complexity, value, viability, cost of error — if any answer is no, stay simpler.</p>
          <h6>If you want that flavour of work anyway</h6>
          <p>Better use of the same hour, and more directly on the blueprint (<strong>Domain 3, 20%</strong>): write a Claude Code slash command or a Skill that runs job-description alignment against your own resume from the terminal. No hosting, no keys, about fifteen minutes.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="hint">
        <summary><span class="h-ico">06</span>Where people will lose the hour (read this one)<span class="h-chev">⌄</span></summary>
        <div class="hint-body">
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Trying to build the whole product.</strong> You are building one loop. No accounts, no saving, no database, no second page.</li>
            <li><strong>Hosting.</strong> This is the classic hour-killer. Tier 2 exists specifically so you never touch a deploy pipeline.</li>
            <li><strong>API keys.</strong> Never put a key in client-side code — not for homework, not ever. If you hit this wall, that's a legitimate finding: say so in Week 5.</li>
            <li><strong>Polishing the interface before the loop works.</strong> Ugly and correct beats beautiful and wrong. Style it in the last ten minutes if there are ten minutes.</li>
            <li><strong>Changing five things at once</strong> when the output is bad, then not knowing which one helped.</li>
          </ul>
          <p><strong>If you're 40 minutes in and it isn't working, stop and submit Tier 1.</strong> Your schema plus an honest account of what broke is a stronger Week 5 contribution than a half-finished app — and "what broke" is the part we'll actually spend time on.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="hint">
        <summary><span class="h-ico">07</span>What to bring to Week 5<span class="h-chev">⌄</span></summary>
        <div class="hint-body">
          <ul class="checklist">
            <li><strong>Your schema.</strong> Every tier has one. This is the primary artifact.</li>
            <li><strong>A link, or a screen recording.</strong> Tier 1 people: your prompt and your before/after outputs.</li>
            <li><strong>One sentence</strong> on which loop you chose and why.</li>
            <li><strong>One sentence</strong> on what broke — and whether you fixed it. "Didn't fix it" is a complete answer.</li>
          </ul>
          <h6>Optional stretch</h6>
          <ul>
            <li>Add an <code>"unclear"</code> value to an enum and find the input that triggers it.</li>
            <li>Make it look like something you'd genuinely put on your portfolio.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

